"Visualizes work items in their actual workflow states together with policies and limits that support controlled flow.\n\nUse when: Coordinate ongoing service work with changing arrivals. Reveal blocked work and excessive work in progress.\n\nAvoid when: The primary need is a fixed project dependency schedule.\n\nVisual grammar: Columns or regions represent defined workflow states. Cards represent bounded work items, with clear commitment and completion points. WIP limits and movement policies constrain how work is pulled.\n\nBuild: Define the actual workflow and work-item boundaries. Agree entry, exit and pull policies plus appropriate WIP limits. Place current items and review blockers, aging work and flow regularly.\n\nCheck: Every card has one unambiguous current workflow state. Limits apply to the regions they actually govern. Done has an explicit delivery or acceptance meaning.\n\nPitfalls: Using columns as departments while obscuring waiting states. Treating every started card as progress despite growing queues.\n\nExample: A repair service limits diagnosis and repair work, marking items blocked by parts so technicians can manage flow deliberately.\n\nAlternatives: Swimlane flowchart, Gantt chart."
